1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:12,240
music

2
00:00:36,346 --> 00:00:37,820
The Crash Test Dummy

3
00:00:37,821 --> 00:00:40,080
Patron Saint of the Motorist

4
00:00:40,081 --> 00:00:44,340
a potent symbol of safety in an age of risk.

5
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,760
He endures countless violent assaults

6
00:00:51,761 --> 00:00:54,840
to help protect our soft, vulnerable bodies

7
00:00:54,841 --> 00:00:58,840
and has become a modern icon

8
00:00:58,841 --> 00:01:01,780
leaving the sterile environment of the lab

9
00:01:01,781 --> 00:01:03,720
to enter the world at large.

10
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:17,440
But as with any star, we see only the public image.

11
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:25,480
Behind his blank-faced innocence lie dark secrets and a strange and gruesome past.

12
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:31,420
Welcome to the secret life of the crash test dump.

13
00:01:31,421 --> 00:01:41,600
I'm trimming up a head and you try to make it look as much like a person as you can.

14
00:01:41,601 --> 00:01:45,140
You take off all the seams, take off all the flash.

15
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:49,600
Like here we have a high seam and I sand it down to make it look nice and smooth.

16
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:54,360
It'll clean it all up and be nice and smooth just like your face.

17
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:59,200
Everybody gets their favorite parts that they like to do.

18
00:01:59,201 --> 00:02:02,000
Vanessa, she likes doing the butts.

19
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:06,100
I like working on the heads and feet and chest.

20
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:11,740
When I first come here, I found it kind of morbid with body parts all over the place.

21
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:16,140
And then after a while, it's like no big deal, you know, but at first it was kind of morbid.

22
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,320
Bringing a fully formed dummy into the world is an expensive business.

23
00:02:25,321 --> 00:02:33,860
A grown adult weighing in at 168 pounds and standing 5 foot 9 inches tall costs about $100,000.

24
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:46,060
He has over 300 individually engineered parts, and every detail of his physiology is determined by law,

25
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:53,300
from the foam in his flesh to the steel in his ribs, even down to his ethnically neutral skin tone.

26
00:02:55,320 --> 00:03:03,100
No new car is allowed on the road until the dummy has given it his thumbs up.

27
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:09,220
He is perhaps the closest relative of the human.

28
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:20,440
Once the dummy has left the factory, he enters a dangerous world.

29
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:23,940
A technological kamikaze.

30
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:31,360
His is a mechanical ballet of destruction, at once brutal and beautiful.

31
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:47,120
The crash test dummy has not always been such a well-known figure.

32
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:52,240
He began his career shrouded in mystery in the deserts of New Mexico.

33
00:03:55,320 --> 00:04:04,600
Here, a strange brew of Cold War paranoia and scientific experimentation came to a head in 1947.

34
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:13,180
The Air Force claimed they'd discovered the crashed remains of a flying saucer.

35
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,100
The episode entered UFO folklore as the Roswell incident.

36
00:04:18,101 --> 00:04:27,780
All the newspapers west of Chicago had a headline that we had captured a flying saucer.

37
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:36,480
There are reports that there were bodies involved, alien beings of some form.

38
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:40,060
Anywhere from three to five.

39
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,720
Descriptions of three and a half to five feet tall.

40
00:04:43,721 --> 00:04:46,860
large heads, big eyes

41
00:04:46,861 --> 00:04:49,160
no ears as we know them

42
00:04:49,161 --> 00:04:51,680
although they had orifices in the side of their head

43
00:04:51,681 --> 00:04:54,260
a small nose, a slit for a mouth

44
00:04:54,261 --> 00:04:56,040
long arms, four fingers

45
00:04:56,041 --> 00:05:01,800
Why would they come to New Mexico?

46
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:04,700
I think one of the key factors to me

47
00:05:04,701 --> 00:05:08,040
100 miles south of Ross

48
00:05:08,041 --> 00:05:10,400
the first atomic bomb in the history of the world

49
00:05:10,401 --> 00:05:11,101
was detonated

50
00:05:11,102 --> 00:05:19,320
We had the V2 rockets of white sands being launched out into space.

51
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:23,700
Rocket awake!

52
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:32,140
Just perhaps they were sitting out there looking and saw this taking place and might have wondered what are these people up to?

53
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:45,860
During the Cold War, everything we did was classified.

54
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:49,040
We didn't want people to know what we were doing.

55
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:56,240
In 1997, shortly before the 50th anniversary of the Roswell incident,

56
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:58,480
the Air Force came out with a document titled,

57
00:05:58,820 --> 00:06:01,160
Roswell Incident, Colon, Case Closed.

58
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:05,420
And one of the things they claimed in there was that anthropomorphic dummies

59
00:06:05,421 --> 00:06:10,840
could have been confused with alien sightings at Roswell in 1947.

60
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:21,220
The crash test dummy had conjured up his first myth.

61
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:25,680
But what was really happening out in the deserts of New Mexico?

62
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:30,040
CID was the first side impact dummy.

63
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:32,328
Developed in the late 1970s by the U.S.

64
00:06:32,329 --> 00:06:34,700
National Highway Traffic Safety Administration,

65
00:06:34,701 --> 00:06:39,220
it is used in U.S. government-required side impact testing of new cars.

66
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:41,720
Modern Marvels will be right back.

67
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:50,720
In 1947, in the deserts of New Mexico,

68
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:53,800
an Air Force colonel named John Stapp

69
00:06:53,801 --> 00:06:57,480
began a bizarre, top-secret aeromedical project

70
00:06:57,481 --> 00:07:01,500
that would lead to the development of the first crash test dummies.

71
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:05,580
Colonel John Paul Stapp is sort of the father of this business

72
00:07:05,581 --> 00:07:09,980
in the sense that he was interested in how to protect air crewmen

73
00:07:09,981 --> 00:07:13,680
when they ejected out of an aircraft at high speed.

74
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:17,900
And he didn't know any way to study that

75
00:07:17,901 --> 00:07:22,080
other than to subject human volunteers to loading that's very similar.

76
00:07:22,081 --> 00:07:22,781
Go!

77
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:29,340
One of the rules of human volunteer testing is

78
00:07:29,341 --> 00:07:31,220
you've got to be willing to do it yourself.

79
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:33,800
So he was his own volunteer.

80
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:39,480
Stepp used this high-speed test track for a terrifying series of experiments.

81
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:48,760
He started the countdown.

82
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:58,520
Then just as he reaches zero, you get this enormous ram against your back.

83
00:07:58,521 --> 00:08:00,620
and feel totally helpless.

84
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:04,240
You're being given the biggest kick that could be delivered.

85
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:15,760
Remember, there was the old saying in the Army,

86
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:17,140
don't volunteer.

87
00:08:18,620 --> 00:08:21,800
And in fact, there were several figures in the early days

88
00:08:21,801 --> 00:08:23,260
of this kind of work.

89
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:27,840
Before, there were dummies who were doing peculiar things.

90
00:08:28,520 --> 00:08:38,260
Several of these people became disabled.

91
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:49,580
Undeterred by the risks, Colonel Stapp pushed human testing to the limit.

92
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:56,640
He was trying to work out the maximum speed at which pilots could safely eject.

93
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:05,760
During a seven-year period, he volunteered for 26 potentially lethal experiments.

94
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:07,340
So Dr.

95
00:09:07,341 --> 00:09:16,420
Stapp kept going faster and faster, and finally in December 1954, he hit his fastest speed.

96
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:24,860
According to the newspaper, 632 miles an hour.

97
00:09:24,861 --> 00:09:29,260
According to the calculations I made from feet per second, at one point he hit 639.

98
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:33,420
But the average would be about 632.

99
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:37,760
And he stopped very, very short.

100
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:40,920
Just a matter of a second or less.

101
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:51,760
Stapp's record-breaking run made him the fastest man on earth.

102
00:09:51,761 --> 00:10:00,960
He endured a massive 43 G's during the deceleration, the equivalent of smashing into a wall at 70 miles per hour.

103
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:08,300
As he said, his eyeballs just about popped out.

104
00:10:09,260 --> 00:10:16,380
His eyes started bleeding and for approximately 10 minutes following his famous run, he could not see.

105
00:10:16,580 --> 00:10:19,280
But later in the hospital, his vision started returning.

106
00:10:19,281 --> 00:10:25,040
He also sustained blisters all over his body.

107
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:30,060
Dust particles impinged upon his skin right through the flight suit he was wearing

108
00:10:30,061 --> 00:10:35,200
and raised rather interesting looking welts, which fortunately went away.

109
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:43,300
Stapp's work was getting too dangerous.

110
00:10:44,580 --> 00:10:46,420
He had to find a substitute.

111
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:53,020
What he needed was a dummy that was a replica human.

112
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:58,380
But his first bionic man was somewhat Neanderthal.

113
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:01,540
The early dummies were very crude.

114
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:06,480
They were just masses that were connected to simulate the weight

115
00:11:06,481 --> 00:11:10,560
and possibly a little bit of the articulation of the human.

116
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:16,440
But in many ways, they were just sandbags to get the mass and the shape.

117
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:21,700
The cabin pressure differentials in excess...

118
00:11:21,701 --> 00:11:26,000
Stapp decided to replace this rather primeval human replica.

119
00:11:34,580 --> 00:11:38,760
He called on the services of an engineer named Sam Alderson.

120
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:44,360
Alderson had been developing artificial limbs

121
00:11:44,361 --> 00:11:48,900
to help the thousands of servicemen mutilated during the Second World War.

122
00:11:49,280 --> 00:12:01,740
So being involved in the replacement parts for a human, I found it very fascinating to go ahead and duplicate the entire human.

123
00:12:01,741 --> 00:12:09,660
The Air Force had a wealth of anthropometric data, which described the height, weight, and build of all its servicemen.

124
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:15,280
Alderson used these measurements to create a model of the perfectly average pilot.

125
00:12:15,281 --> 00:12:17,728
This is a very old dummy.

126
00:12:17,729 --> 00:12:23,800
It should have its 50th birthday very soon, a couple of years.

127
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:29,380
This dummy itself was used for testing ejection seats.

128
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:32,133
Here was a cavity.

129
00:12:32,134 --> 00:12:40,040
Within this were instruments that radioed back the data to a ground station.

130
00:12:40,041 --> 00:12:43,000
we have duplicated here

131
00:12:43,001 --> 00:12:46,360
some but not all of the motions of the human

132
00:12:46,361 --> 00:12:51,160
these had to be adjusted so that they would respond

133
00:12:51,161 --> 00:12:54,860
to the shocks of ejection

134
00:12:54,861 --> 00:13:08,861
These proto-humans began to replace people in the myriad of experiments taking place in New Mexico.

135
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:28,400
Research into ejection seats meant the Air Force now knew how to get pilots safely out of their aircrafts.

136
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:32,520
The next problem was getting them back to Earth.

137
00:13:32,521 --> 00:13:46,521
They found that if you visualize an axis through your navel, you can rotate around that axis up to 435 revolutions per minute, which is fatal to the human being.

138
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:57,750
So they had to devise some other method, and eventually they did it with dummies, and these dummies would be released at high altitude, and different types of

139
00:13:57,751 --> 00:14:02,320
parachutes would be used to test them when they fell out of the balloon.

140
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:12,440
But one experiment encountered unusual problems over the desert town of Alamogordo.

141
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:20,420
Radio station KALG was playing a musical tune in the FM frequency called Tiger Rag.

142
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:25,180
Unfortunately, one of the harmonic overtones from that radio station

143
00:14:25,181 --> 00:14:28,480
exactly matched the release signal for the dummies.

144
00:14:32,520 --> 00:14:42,080
The dummies were released and they came down in their parachute,

145
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:44,080
landing in the drive-in theater.

146
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:47,740
That was the first alien invasion of Alamogordo.

147
00:14:55,740 --> 00:15:01,840
The arrival of the crash test dummy would revolutionize the military testing program,

148
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:06,200
Practically every piece of equipment now had to pass through his hands

149
00:15:06,201 --> 00:15:08,600
before a human was allowed near it.

150
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:12,180
The dummy had come of age.

151
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,920
Finally, it was time for him to leave the wide open space of the desert,

152
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:19,940
drawn by the lure of the big city.

153
00:15:22,660 --> 00:15:27,620
Developed in 1970, Sierra Susie was one of the first female crash test dummies.

154
00:15:27,621 --> 00:15:32,780
The Sierra Dummy family includes Sam, Stan, Saul, Sue, Sammy, and Toddler.

155
00:15:33,320 --> 00:15:35,180
Modern Marvel will return in a moment.

156
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:40,680
A new era was beckoning for the crash test dummy.

157
00:15:43,980 --> 00:15:52,580
It was time for him to leave the desert wastes of New Mexico for the far north of the USA and the vast metropolis of Detroit.

158
00:15:52,581 --> 00:15:59,360
But it would be a while before the Motor City would welcome the wandering hero.

159
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:14,120
Colonel Stapp's interest in parachute testing was waning.

160
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:18,440
Events closer to the ground had begun to attract his attention.

161
00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:24,660
While reviewing Air Force accident statistics, he made an extraordinary discovery.

162
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:32,520
The majority of serious accidents suffered by pilots, he found, didn't take place in the air at all,

163
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:38,480
but in the short journeys to and from the air base that the pilots made in their cars.

164
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:42,880
And the problem wasn't unique to pilots.

165
00:16:42,881 --> 00:16:52,740
all over the usa more and more motorists were meeting their deaths on the roads there was

166
00:16:52,741 --> 00:16:59,780
the realization that the number of people being killed on the highways was growing rapidly as

167
00:16:59,781 --> 00:17:07,000
more and more people were driving and the projections were if the sales continued that

168
00:17:07,001 --> 00:17:09,040
there'd be very high death rates.

169
00:17:13,140 --> 00:17:16,000
Stapp became obsessed with car safety

170
00:17:16,001 --> 00:17:20,480
and decided to try out his military mannequins in the driving seat.

171
00:17:28,760 --> 00:17:32,380
But cars were a new and alien environment for the dummy,

172
00:17:32,800 --> 00:17:35,960
and it was difficult to interpret the results he was getting.

173
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:48,020
Stapp needed to develop a dummy based on actual car crash data from real living subjects.

174
00:17:48,460 --> 00:17:51,000
But it was far too dangerous to use humans.

175
00:17:51,500 --> 00:17:55,240
So Stapp began his research using pigs as test animals.

176
00:17:55,760 --> 00:18:01,620
Pigs were used, hogs, and they were anesthetized so that the pig wouldn't feel anything.

177
00:18:01,621 --> 00:18:07,820
After all, the anatomy of a pig is similar to that of some human beings.

178
00:18:08,220 --> 00:18:13,760
And the pig would be in the swing seat, and they would see what would happen to the flesh.

179
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:20,720
The information gathered from the injuries sustained by the pig could be fed into the design of the dummy's ribs.

180
00:18:26,060 --> 00:18:30,040
When news of the project leaked out, people were horrified.

181
00:18:30,041 --> 00:18:33,920
but not primarily because of a concern for the pigs.

182
00:18:35,240 --> 00:18:39,240
Putting safety in the spotlight had shown them for the first time

183
00:18:39,241 --> 00:18:42,200
how dangerous driving their cars could be.

184
00:18:43,320 --> 00:18:47,380
Whenever you talk about the automobile industry, you're talking big numbers.

185
00:18:47,381 --> 00:18:52,200
You're talking enormous profits, enormous sales, many, many billions of dollars.

186
00:18:52,660 --> 00:18:55,080
And whenever you have that concentration of money,

187
00:18:55,480 --> 00:18:59,500
they're going to move to protect it and maximize their profits, not maximize safety.

188
00:19:00,040 --> 00:19:08,380
under pressure the Pentagon pulled the plug on Stapp's test program

189
00:19:08,381 --> 00:19:13,460
but over the next few years it became clear

190
00:19:13,461 --> 00:19:16,060
that the problem could not be ignored forever

191
00:19:16,061 --> 00:19:26,460
in 1966

192
00:19:26,461 --> 00:19:31,860
Congress passed a series of laws making car crash testing a legal requirement.

193
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:38,520
The hunt for the perfect artificial human was officially on, and there was some way to go.

194
00:19:39,020 --> 00:19:42,140
When crash test dummies started to be used in the auto industry,

195
00:19:42,780 --> 00:19:45,820
they were an adaptation of the military dummies,

196
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:49,840
and they were used initially pretty much for ballast.

197
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:52,660
When they were crash tested, they were just in there for weight.

198
00:19:56,460 --> 00:20:01,080
But as interest began to accrue in terms of protecting the occupants,

199
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:05,980
questions began to be asked about, well, how realistic are these dummies

200
00:20:05,981 --> 00:20:09,220
and can we make measurements that might relate to injury?

201
00:20:46,220 --> 00:20:51,380
The motor industry tried all kinds of new designs for the crash test dummy.

202
00:20:51,381 --> 00:21:00,980
they soon realized that they needed complex and detailed information about the real human body

203
00:21:00,981 --> 00:21:04,760
you need to develop what are called injury criteria

204
00:21:04,761 --> 00:21:13,280
and you're going to use the dummy to measure these criteria can be forces can be accelerations

205
00:21:13,281 --> 00:21:16,700
and then these criteria should relate to injury in the human

206
00:21:16,701 --> 00:21:22,580
so it might take you know a couple hundred pounds to fracture something in the human

207
00:21:22,581 --> 00:21:27,020
and you'd measure that in the dummy and that would predict injury in the human they had to

208
00:21:27,021 --> 00:21:33,580
test something that behaved just like a living human being without risking human life their

209
00:21:33,581 --> 00:21:42,340
solution the human corpse it's known in the trade as cadaver testing i remember when we were looking

210
00:21:42,341 --> 00:21:45,760
at testing cadavers at first, I went and observed an autopsy,

211
00:21:45,800 --> 00:21:48,000
and I remember I had to sit down in the middle of the autopsy

212
00:21:48,001 --> 00:21:52,220
just because of, you know, so many sites that I hadn't observed before.

213
00:21:52,720 --> 00:21:55,160
And I think you do get desensitized.

214
00:21:55,240 --> 00:21:57,600
What you don't get desensitized to, though, is the respect

215
00:21:57,601 --> 00:22:00,500
and sort of the research you're doing and the importance of it.

216
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:08,460
It takes a lot of work to prepare a cadaver,

217
00:22:08,461 --> 00:22:13,260
or as some researchers call them, post-mortem human surrogates.

218
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:17,220
Following death, there's a number of changes that take place in the body.

219
00:22:17,620 --> 00:22:20,120
And one of these that most people are familiar with is rigor mortis,

220
00:22:20,540 --> 00:22:24,100
basically some chemical changes that make the body very stiff and tense.

221
00:22:24,700 --> 00:22:27,600
However, this wears off after a certain period of time.

222
00:22:27,601 --> 00:22:31,320
So it'll go through a tense period, and then ultimately it'll relax again.

223
00:22:32,220 --> 00:22:35,680
We actually do something that's called exercising of cadaver's joints,

224
00:22:35,681 --> 00:22:38,840
where we'll actually move the joints through a full range of motion

225
00:22:38,841 --> 00:22:40,840
in order to break up some of this stiffness.

226
00:22:42,520 --> 00:22:45,740
We try and replicate the living condition as well as we can.

227
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:49,980
In the cardiovascular system, the heart, the aorta,

228
00:22:50,220 --> 00:22:51,680
we actually inject in a fluid.

229
00:22:52,960 --> 00:22:56,480
You can't really bruise a cadaver, but we put a dye in.

230
00:22:56,680 --> 00:22:58,700
And so if there's ruptures in some of the vessels,

231
00:22:58,980 --> 00:23:01,420
you'll see that this dye actually stains the tissue

232
00:23:01,421 --> 00:23:04,540
and would be similar to what a rupture or an approximation

233
00:23:04,541 --> 00:23:05,960
of what you'd get for bruising.

234
00:23:07,480 --> 00:23:09,560
Once the corpse has been prepared,

235
00:23:10,040 --> 00:23:12,300
measuring instruments are fitted for the test.

236
00:23:14,020 --> 00:23:18,200
What we would do is we would put instrumentation into the cadaver,

237
00:23:18,440 --> 00:23:19,960
and this would be various sensors,

238
00:23:20,320 --> 00:23:23,820
load cells, accelerometers, strain gauges,

239
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:25,980
and these are attached to bone generally.

240
00:23:26,180 --> 00:23:29,620
So we would actually go in and we would screw or attach with wire

241
00:23:29,621 --> 00:23:32,400
these accelerometers and load cells to the bone itself.

242
00:23:34,540 --> 00:23:41,640
In cadaver tests like these,

243
00:23:41,920 --> 00:23:47,380
the dead body gave scientists precise measurements

244
00:23:47,381 --> 00:23:51,660
of the forces needed to break bones and rupture blood vessels.

245
00:23:53,340 --> 00:23:55,700
You need cadavers to develop dummies.

246
00:23:55,701 --> 00:23:57,480
There's a tremendous amount of cadaver research

247
00:23:57,481 --> 00:24:00,500
that has gone into developing every component within the dummy.

248
00:24:04,540 --> 00:24:11,740
This cadaver test shows some of the problems of reproducing the behavior of the human body in a dummy.

249
00:24:13,420 --> 00:24:18,620
At the moment of impact, the neck stretches to try and accommodate the shock.

250
00:24:22,020 --> 00:24:27,840
The forces here are so extreme that it can't stretch enough, and the neck breaks.

251
00:24:27,841 --> 00:24:38,440
this is very complex behavior for an engineer to reproduce in a crash test dummy and the neck is

252
00:24:38,441 --> 00:24:45,000
just one example the human body is made up of hundreds of different moving parts and materials

253
00:24:45,001 --> 00:24:53,840
if you look at the human body it's probably the greatest engineering structure

254
00:24:53,841 --> 00:24:57,260
I just marvel at the complexity

255
00:24:57,261 --> 00:24:58,320
and some of the designs

256
00:24:58,321 --> 00:24:59,540
the frictionless joints

257
00:24:59,541 --> 00:25:00,700
and some of the details

258
00:25:00,701 --> 00:25:02,760
it's a very resilient structure

259
00:25:02,761 --> 00:25:04,400
it's an engineering marvel

260
00:25:04,401 --> 00:25:05,260
that's been developed

261
00:25:05,261 --> 00:25:07,500
to accommodate loads and forces

262
00:25:07,501 --> 00:25:08,660
through everyday life

263
00:25:08,661 --> 00:25:09,900
as well as circumstances

264
00:25:09,901 --> 00:25:11,140
that hopefully don't happen everyday

265
00:25:11,141 --> 00:25:12,240
like a car crash

266
00:25:12,241 --> 00:25:15,440
the perfect dummy

267
00:25:15,441 --> 00:25:17,300
would somehow have to replicate

268
00:25:17,301 --> 00:25:18,860
the fantastic complexity

269
00:25:18,861 --> 00:25:20,420
of the real human body

270
00:25:20,421 --> 00:25:31,720
it makes a big difference whether you hit bone fast or slow because if you hit it fast

271
00:25:31,721 --> 00:25:38,620
it doesn't have time to accommodate that fast hit as it would do if you hit it slow

272
00:25:38,621 --> 00:25:46,800
and therefore the speed of impact has a very significant effect on the way that the bone responds

273
00:25:46,801 --> 00:25:55,260
Dummy engineers wrestled with these problems as they tried to perfect their artificial human.

274
00:26:00,160 --> 00:26:03,480
By the early 70s, they believed they'd done it.

275
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:11,700
All the developments and designs came together in the shape of a single dummy, the Hybrid 3.

276
00:26:16,800 --> 00:26:22,040
Crash test dummy experiments led to safer cars.

277
00:26:22,420 --> 00:26:27,780
In 1973, the first passenger car to be sold with an airbag was the Oldsmobile Toronado.

278
00:26:28,240 --> 00:26:30,797
And in 1988, Chrysler became the first U.S.

279
00:26:30,798 --> 00:26:34,780
auto manufacturer to make driver's side airbags standard equipment.

280
00:26:35,440 --> 00:26:37,680
Modern models will return in a moment.

281
00:26:37,681 --> 00:26:50,620
the hybrid 3 crash test dummy developed in the early 70s is still in use today and he remains

282
00:26:50,621 --> 00:26:56,620
the only legally recognized dummy of his kind the movement is pretty good this range of motion is

283
00:26:56,621 --> 00:27:04,440
representative of the actual human and the movement of the neck is much like ours and he can twist

284
00:27:04,441 --> 00:27:07,440
It also moves side to side.

285
00:27:07,441 --> 00:27:11,440
And every movement that we're doing is registering on the load cell,

286
00:27:11,441 --> 00:27:15,440
and we can tell how much force it actually took to move.

287
00:27:15,441 --> 00:27:19,440
We plug in all the instruments into the data acquisition system,

288
00:27:19,441 --> 00:27:22,440
which is the computer for the dummy.

289
00:27:22,441 --> 00:27:25,440
We record the information, and then after the crash,

290
00:27:25,441 --> 00:27:28,440
we can download this information from the data acquisition system.

291
00:27:28,441 --> 00:27:32,440
And he'll let us know how well he did in the crash

292
00:27:32,441 --> 00:27:33,800
show how he's feeling afterwards.

293
00:27:36,740 --> 00:27:40,180
Every new car has to be driven by the Hybrid 3

294
00:27:40,181 --> 00:27:43,000
before a human is allowed to take the wheel.

295
00:27:56,980 --> 00:27:58,640
The car is crashed.

296
00:27:59,400 --> 00:28:01,740
The sensors in the dummy send out readings

297
00:28:01,741 --> 00:28:06,480
telling the computer the exact force that each part of the dummy has endured.

298
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:11,540
If the readings fall within limits prescribed by the law, the car is passed.

299
00:28:12,020 --> 00:28:14,980
It can then leave the lab and enter the showroom.

300
00:28:31,740 --> 00:28:41,700
As cars became safer and safer, the march of the dummy continued.

301
00:28:46,640 --> 00:28:53,480
One of the feelings that people had about dummies, and they stood for actually the ability to soak up punishment,

302
00:28:53,481 --> 00:29:02,260
And the ordinary driver found it reassuring that someone had been through that particular mill before he had to go through it.

303
00:29:05,060 --> 00:29:11,380
The crash test dummy escaped from the test labs of the car companies into the world at large,

304
00:29:11,720 --> 00:29:15,040
becoming a familiar and friendly icon to popular culture.

305
00:29:17,520 --> 00:29:22,100
He was given star billing in pop promos and advertising campaigns.

306
00:29:23,480 --> 00:29:30,480
Everyone, it seemed, had fallen for the dummy.

307
00:29:39,860 --> 00:29:44,160
No longer was he confused with visitors from another planet,

308
00:29:44,520 --> 00:29:49,240
nor was he a secret and possibly even sinister tool of the military.

309
00:29:49,740 --> 00:29:53,460
He had become the symbol of a caring, risk-free age.

310
00:29:53,900 --> 00:29:56,180
He was the human face of technology.

311
00:30:00,680 --> 00:30:05,060
We are the dummies crashing on the roof, busting our heads, just for you.

312
00:30:05,280 --> 00:30:07,240
My name is Vince, the job makes me sore.

313
00:30:07,440 --> 00:30:09,380
If you'd buckle up, I wouldn't do it no more.

314
00:30:09,381 --> 00:30:11,640
They call me Larry. I'm a dummy, too.

315
00:30:11,860 --> 00:30:13,780
When you don't buckle up, your dummy is you.

316
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:18,140
In the USA, two crash test dummies called Vince and Larry

317
00:30:18,141 --> 00:30:23,380
became national celebrities thanks to a decade-long road safety campaign.

318
00:30:23,381 --> 00:30:24,880
Put your safety belt on.

319
00:30:26,440 --> 00:30:30,540
At the time, U.S. belt use rate was about 11%, very low.

320
00:30:30,660 --> 00:30:33,000
So we wanted to do something in the public service arena

321
00:30:33,001 --> 00:30:36,180
to get people's attention to encourage them to wear their safety belts.

322
00:30:36,480 --> 00:30:40,260
And they did some research and found out that fear wasn't really a good motivator.

323
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:43,980
People didn't really want to see blood and guts to motivate them to buckle up.

324
00:30:44,800 --> 00:30:46,380
Even with their bags, Vince...

325
00:30:46,381 --> 00:30:48,200
We found that it would actually be a turnoff.

326
00:30:48,360 --> 00:30:51,440
So they came up with humor as the way to reach the audience,

327
00:30:51,540 --> 00:30:52,920
and Vince and Larry were born.

328
00:30:53,100 --> 00:30:53,960
You were right, Vince.

329
00:30:54,180 --> 00:30:54,880
Say what?

330
00:30:54,881 --> 00:30:57,560
When you hit a wall going 50 and you're not wearing a safety belt,

331
00:30:57,720 --> 00:30:59,380
your head will fit in the glove box.

332
00:30:59,760 --> 00:31:01,180
We call them icons.

333
00:31:01,181 --> 00:31:04,220
They are as well known as Smokey the Bear, Tony the Tiger,

334
00:31:04,340 --> 00:31:05,520
and the Pillsbury Doughboys.

335
00:31:05,780 --> 00:31:08,660
You say Vince and Larry, everybody knows exactly who you're talking about.

336
00:31:09,900 --> 00:31:12,200
If we'd had half a brain, we'd have buckled up.

337
00:31:12,240 --> 00:31:14,200
Hey, bumper breath, I do have half a brain.

338
00:31:16,380 --> 00:31:25,300
The dummy gained in popularity, becoming a cult personality around the world.

339
00:31:25,920 --> 00:31:33,700
But despite the flattery of the advertisers, some serious limitations of the crash test dummy were beginning to emerge.

340
00:31:33,701 --> 00:31:44,420
Although scientists had created a whole family of dummies, including women and children,

341
00:31:44,980 --> 00:31:51,240
the only dummy required by law to guarantee car safety was based on the average male.

342
00:31:52,280 --> 00:31:55,960
The primary crash test dummy is the 50th percentile male.

343
00:31:56,360 --> 00:32:01,900
And that dummy represents the average 5'9", about 160-pound male,

344
00:32:01,901 --> 00:32:05,040
and doesn't represent anybody else.

345
00:32:05,240 --> 00:32:08,900
But every single safety standard is geared to the average male.

346
00:32:09,300 --> 00:32:12,860
So if you're a petite woman of about 4'8",

347
00:32:12,861 --> 00:32:15,860
who weighs 100 pounds, or a small child,

348
00:32:16,060 --> 00:32:17,080
you're not protected.

349
00:32:17,840 --> 00:32:20,440
There was one part of the population in particular

350
00:32:20,441 --> 00:32:23,600
that suffered from the fact that the legal standards

351
00:32:23,601 --> 00:32:27,460
revolved around the so-called 50th percentile male.

352
00:32:28,260 --> 00:32:30,620
If you look at where the largest needs are right now

353
00:32:30,621 --> 00:32:32,760
in terms of developing dummies, improving dummies.

354
00:32:33,100 --> 00:32:35,720
It's certainly in terms of child dummies.

355
00:32:36,160 --> 00:32:38,880
And if you look at some of the big problems that have come up the past couple years,

356
00:32:38,980 --> 00:32:41,820
certainly in the United States, it's been children in airbags,

357
00:32:41,940 --> 00:32:44,920
out-of-position children in close proximity to a deploying airbag.

358
00:32:54,040 --> 00:32:56,900
Not only have children failed to be protected,

359
00:32:56,901 --> 00:33:00,580
they have actually been killed by these safety devices.

360
00:33:00,620 --> 00:33:10,080
More than 80 children have died because of airbags exploding in their faces

361
00:33:10,081 --> 00:33:21,260
One of the problems is that child dummies are merely scaled down versions of the adult dummies

362
00:33:21,261 --> 00:33:26,080
In real life, the biological relationship is far more complex

363
00:33:26,081 --> 00:33:34,040
Building a realistic child dummy requires data from real children,

364
00:33:34,640 --> 00:33:39,320
data that can be obtained only from crash tests using children's corpses.

365
00:33:40,680 --> 00:33:44,600
If there were child cadaver data available at this time,

366
00:33:44,800 --> 00:33:47,000
it would allow us to make a safer dummy,

367
00:33:47,300 --> 00:33:50,200
it would allow us to design safer airbag systems,

368
00:33:50,420 --> 00:33:54,220
and ultimately you would see that as a reduction in terms of the number of fatalities

369
00:33:54,221 --> 00:33:55,800
that are resulting on the roads.

370
00:33:56,080 --> 00:33:58,120
But getting this data is not easy.

371
00:34:00,660 --> 00:34:03,320
Scientists at Heidelberg University in Germany

372
00:34:03,321 --> 00:34:07,820
carried out a series of experiments using the cadavers of children

373
00:34:07,821 --> 00:34:11,660
in order to obtain this much needed biomechanical information.

374
00:34:13,660 --> 00:34:16,960
But the research project turned into a scandal.

375
00:34:18,440 --> 00:34:21,620
German tabloids uncovered details of the experiments,

376
00:34:21,621 --> 00:34:24,720
dubbing Dr. Calieres Professor Horror.

377
00:34:24,721 --> 00:34:33,480
The outrage led to a public inquiry.

378
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:40,640
The lab in Heidelberg was closed down, and further experiments on cadavers of children were halted.

379
00:34:41,540 --> 00:34:46,900
Krabby, the child-restraint airbag interaction dummy, was developed to help make airbags safe for children.

380
00:34:47,360 --> 00:34:51,680
They're available in three sizes, 18-month-old, 12-month-old, and 6-month-old.

381
00:34:51,681 --> 00:34:54,160
Modern Marvel will return in a moment.

382
00:34:54,720 --> 00:35:08,720
The crash test dummy has an insatiable appetite for data from real human beings.

383
00:35:13,760 --> 00:35:18,940
Only by building up this data can the dummy develop to fit his changing environment.

384
00:35:18,941 --> 00:35:26,920
The nature of cars and car crashes is constantly shifting

385
00:35:26,921 --> 00:35:29,100
and so the dummy must mutate.

386
00:35:29,460 --> 00:35:33,240
He must eval in order to stay true to the people he is replicating.

387
00:35:37,400 --> 00:35:41,780
In the march of the dummy, it is the survival of the most human.

388
00:35:41,781 --> 00:35:52,120
The Hybrid 3 is now more than 25 years old.

389
00:35:52,780 --> 00:35:56,480
Some in the road safety community think he is an endangered species.

390
00:36:01,620 --> 00:36:06,600
Because the airbag has come in and restraint system usage is up,

391
00:36:06,601 --> 00:36:10,600
the combination of those two devices has changed injury patterns.

392
00:36:11,780 --> 00:36:19,320
There are less fatal injuries occurring,

393
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:27,580
and as a result, situations where the occupant would go immediately to the morgue,

394
00:36:27,940 --> 00:36:29,800
they're now going to the emergency room,

395
00:36:29,801 --> 00:36:31,720
and they're finding now that they're not dead,

396
00:36:31,860 --> 00:36:35,760
they have to deal with these very devastating sometimes lower extremity injuries.

397
00:36:37,040 --> 00:36:40,120
The types of injury that we're particularly concerned with

398
00:36:40,121 --> 00:36:44,420
They're not perhaps the commonest, which are injuries just to the edge of the ankle in the foot,

399
00:36:44,480 --> 00:36:47,980
but those that cause significant disability and long-term morbidity.

400
00:36:48,680 --> 00:36:52,580
This bone down here, the calcaneum, the talus, this bone in the middle of the ankle,

401
00:36:53,040 --> 00:36:57,160
and the tibia itself are quite often destroyed in frontal impact in car crashes.

402
00:36:58,260 --> 00:37:01,080
These fractures are difficult to repair for us as surgeons.

403
00:37:01,081 --> 00:37:05,920
They require long operations, long rehabilitative periods, lots of physiotherapy,

404
00:37:06,340 --> 00:37:09,440
and an extraordinarily long time before patients can walk again afterwards.

405
00:37:10,120 --> 00:37:21,080
Tests were carried out on amputated legs to analyze these new injuries.

406
00:37:22,400 --> 00:37:28,500
Tests have included tests at speed to look at the way the leg bends at the ankle when it's hit

407
00:37:28,501 --> 00:37:31,460
and the way that force is transmitted through the foot

408
00:37:31,461 --> 00:37:35,740
and indeed through these bones that are most often broken and cause such disabling injuries.

409
00:37:35,741 --> 00:37:41,820
and the answer to these new problems

410
00:37:41,821 --> 00:37:52,460
Thor, the test device for human occupant restraint

411
00:37:52,461 --> 00:37:56,480
the crash test dummy for the 21st century

412
00:38:05,740 --> 00:38:14,260
Thor is the result of nearly 20 years research.

413
00:38:16,120 --> 00:38:20,560
He is an altogether more sophisticated creation than earlier dummies.

414
00:38:27,700 --> 00:38:31,300
Mark Hafner has been working on Thor for the last decade.

415
00:38:32,420 --> 00:38:34,860
Let me take the cover off so you can see it a little better.

416
00:38:35,740 --> 00:38:38,400
This is a part of the dummy we were really very proud of.

417
00:38:38,480 --> 00:38:39,600
It's our most recent addition.

418
00:38:40,180 --> 00:38:43,040
This is the representation of the human tibia bone from top to bottom.

419
00:38:43,740 --> 00:38:50,280
The ankle basically has information designed into it to mimic the motion of the ankle,

420
00:38:50,440 --> 00:38:52,380
what we call inversion and inversion, which is this motion,

421
00:38:52,840 --> 00:38:56,900
and dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, which is this motion.

422
00:38:58,360 --> 00:39:03,380
In addition, we've incorporated this cable coming back to the back of the heel.

423
00:39:03,381 --> 00:39:07,720
This represents the load path of the Achilles tendon

424
00:39:07,721 --> 00:39:13,420
and is basically activated when the foot is moved in this direction.

425
00:39:16,180 --> 00:39:18,860
Thor is much more human than his forebears.

426
00:39:19,540 --> 00:39:21,180
The neck is multidirectional.

427
00:39:21,980 --> 00:39:26,020
The ribs are angled to give them a more human-like response on impact.

428
00:39:26,580 --> 00:39:30,080
The curve of the spine, too, is much closer to that of the human,

429
00:39:30,081 --> 00:39:33,000
so the dummy can adopt any seating position.

430
00:39:33,380 --> 00:39:38,760
Thor also contains a special viscera sac, which mimics the response of the internal organs.

431
00:39:39,240 --> 00:39:41,940
Its buttocks even produce a realistic seed print.

432
00:39:48,020 --> 00:39:51,120
But Thor might never see the light of day.

433
00:39:56,420 --> 00:40:00,540
Crash test dummies as a species are in danger of extinction.

434
00:40:03,380 --> 00:40:09,740
They are threatened by a new contender, the virtual human.

435
00:40:11,360 --> 00:40:20,560
There's no question that in the very near future, we will have a computer human that is better than anything.

436
00:40:20,561 --> 00:40:29,720
I think that 10 years from now

437
00:40:29,721 --> 00:40:33,020
we'll have a first class computer model

438
00:40:33,021 --> 00:40:34,100
of the human

439
00:40:34,101 --> 00:40:37,860
and we'll be running most of the automobile

440
00:40:37,861 --> 00:40:39,780
crashes on the computer

441
00:40:39,781 --> 00:40:46,680
People that are going to be in a frontal crash

442
00:40:46,681 --> 00:40:48,760
approximately two thirds of them are bracing

443
00:40:48,761 --> 00:40:51,040
So they're tensing, they're pressing on the brake pedal,

444
00:40:51,520 --> 00:40:56,100
and obviously we have a great deal of difficulty replicating this in a cadaver specimen.

445
00:40:56,800 --> 00:41:00,900
So what we try and do, we've tried various techniques, we've tried external springs,

446
00:41:01,240 --> 00:41:05,340
we've tried all sorts of ideas, but we really don't have anything that's successful.

447
00:41:05,900 --> 00:41:09,080
And here's where things like computer modeling, for example,

448
00:41:09,460 --> 00:41:12,240
you can actually incorporate the musculature into the model.

449
00:41:12,241 --> 00:41:20,180
In one sense, the virtual human is a completely new species.

450
00:41:22,240 --> 00:41:25,380
Crashes in cyberspace are perfectly repeatable.

451
00:41:26,100 --> 00:41:27,620
They don't need real cars.

452
00:41:28,020 --> 00:41:29,480
They are safe and clean.

453
00:41:30,180 --> 00:41:33,560
But he has inherited one important habit from his ancestors.

454
00:41:42,240 --> 00:41:56,240
In Detroit, scientists have once again turned for information to the human corpse.

455
00:41:59,800 --> 00:42:03,560
In an effort to reduce the number of head injuries caused by car crashes,

456
00:42:04,180 --> 00:42:06,600
researchers have been modeling the human brain.

457
00:42:07,780 --> 00:42:10,840
Essentially, we are looking at actual deformations within the brain

458
00:42:10,841 --> 00:42:18,260
and doing extensive modeling work in an effort to come up with perhaps a new and improved way

459
00:42:18,261 --> 00:42:23,020
of predicting what the potential for head injury in any given situation,

460
00:42:23,360 --> 00:42:25,280
specifically crash scenarios, might be.

461
00:42:25,900 --> 00:42:28,260
This is one of the primary components of our research.

462
00:42:28,261 --> 00:42:32,700
This is a triaxial neutral density accelerometer.

463
00:42:33,660 --> 00:42:38,440
More commonly, it's simply a plastic box with some foam in it

464
00:42:38,441 --> 00:42:41,860
and three very small acceleration measurement chips.

465
00:42:42,800 --> 00:42:46,700
And they are mounted in such a way that we can measure acceleration

466
00:42:46,701 --> 00:42:49,960
in three directions that are perpendicular to each other.

467
00:42:54,120 --> 00:42:59,400
This means removing the head from a corpse and inserting probes into the brain.

468
00:43:03,660 --> 00:43:07,440
The head is then mounted on the crash test rig and impacted.

469
00:43:08,440 --> 00:43:18,060
An ultra-high-speed X-ray camera analyzes the movement of the brain tissue.

470
00:43:24,220 --> 00:43:28,080
The information from the corpse is fed into the computer model.

471
00:43:30,740 --> 00:43:34,760
It will help to create the most authentic replica human yet.

472
00:43:38,440 --> 00:43:43,420
At 52 years of age, the crash test dummy is on his deathbed.

473
00:43:47,880 --> 00:43:52,600
His impassive expression gives no sign of his star-studded existence,

474
00:43:53,280 --> 00:43:55,880
nor does it betray his darkest secrets.

475
00:43:55,881 --> 00:44:08,580
for this replica human who has survived a million crashes

476
00:44:08,581 --> 00:44:11,020
and saved the lives of thousands

477
00:44:11,021 --> 00:44:20,640
it is finally time to face his own mortality

478
00:44:25,880 --> 00:44:29,911
Our president was gone. Generals were in rebellion.

479
00:44:29,912 --> 00:44:33,704
In 30 days, we would cease to exist as a nation.

480
00:44:33,705 --> 00:44:39,869
But a handshake and one man's word changed history forever. It wasn't a month.

481
00:44:39,870 --> 00:44:45,480
It was a miracle. April 1865. Monday at 9. Only on the History Channel.

